COMMAND NAME: gpmigrator

Upgrades an existing Greenplum Database 3.3.x.x system to 4.0.x.x.

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpmigrator <old_GPHOME_path> <new_GPHOME_path> 
           --replication_port_base <port> 
           --mirror_replication_port_base <port> 
          [--mirror-mode redundant|single] 
          [--nosnapshot] [-d <master_data_directory>] 
          [-l <logfile_directory>] [-q] [--debug] [-R]

gpmigrator --prepare 
          [--master_port <port>] 
          [--port_base <port>] [--mirror_port_base <port>] 
          [--replication_port_base <port>] 
          [--mirror_replication_port_base <port>] 
           <old_GPHOME_path> <new_GPHOME_path> 
          [-d <master_data_directory>] [-l <logfile_directory>] 
          [-q] [--debug]

gpmigrator <old_GPHOME_path> <new_GPHOME_path> --resume

gpmigrator --version | -v

gpmigrator --help | -h


*****************************************************
PREREQUISITES
*****************************************************

The following tasks should be performed prior to executing an upgrade:

 * Make sure you are logged in to the master host as the Greenplum 
   Database superuser (gpadmin).

 * Install the Greenplum Database 4.0 binaries on all Greenplum hosts.

 * Copy any custom modules from your existing installation to your 4.0 
   installation on all Greenplum hosts. For example, shared library files 
   for user-defined functions in $GPHOME/lib or PostgreSQL add-on modules 
   (such as plr.so or pgcrypto.so) in $GPHOME/lib/postgresql.

 * Copy or preserve any additional folders or files (such as backup folders) 
   that you have added in the Greenplum data directories or $GPHOME directory. 
   Only files or folders strictly related to Greenplum Database operations 
   are preserved by the migration utility.

 * (Optional) Run VACUUM on all databases, and remove old server log files 
   from pg_log in your master and segment data directories. This is not 
   required, but will reduce the size of Greenplum Database files to be 
   backed up and migrated.

 * Remove the gp_jetpack schema from all databases (if it exists). Also 
   note that gp_ is a reserved prefix in Greenplum Database 4.0 (as is pg_). 
   If you have named any objects (databases, schemas, tables, functions) 
   with a gp_ prefix, you must rename them prior to upgrading.
 
 * The use of OIDs (per-row system-generated object identifiers) has been 
   disabled for user-created tables in 4.0. If you have tables that were 
   created with OIDS=TRUE, you must alter them to be with OIDS=FALSE.

 * Stop Greenplum Performance Monitor (if running) using gpperfmon --stop.

 * Check for and recover any failed segments in your current Greenplum 
   Database system (gpstate, gprecoverseg).

 * Verify the integrity of your current system catalogs using the --prepare 
   step of gpmigrator. This will check your system catalogs and indexes and 
   initialize a temporary 4.0 system. If problems are found, contact 
   Greenplum customer support. 

 * Backup your current databases (gpcrondump or ZFS snapshots). If you 
   find any issues when testing your upgraded system, you can restore this 
   backup.

 * Remove the standby master from your system configuration 
   (gpinitstandby -r).

 * Do a clean shutdown of your current system (gpstop).

 * Update your environment to source the 4.0 installation.

 * Inform all database users of the upgrade and lockout time frame. Once 
   the upgrade is in process, users will not be allowed on the system 
   until the upgrade is complete.

WARNING: Failing to meet any of these prerequisites can result in 
         an aborted upgrade, placing your system in an unusable or 
         even unrecoverable state. Make sure you meet all the 
         prerequisites before running gpmigrator.


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpmigrator utility upgrades an existing Greenplum Database 
3.3.x.x system to 4.0. This utility updates the system catalog 
and internal version number, but not the actual software binaries.
During the migration process, all client connections to the master 
will be locked out. The migration utility locks all client access 
to the master, however it does not block direct utility mode access 
to the segments. To ensure a safe upgrade, make sure users are aware 
that any connections to a Greenplum segment are not safe during the 
upgrade time frame.

Prior to executing the upgrade, you can optionally run gpmigrator 
in prepare mode to perform some pre-upgrade validation without 
actually executing the upgrade. The prepare phase does three things; 
it runs a utility called gpcheckcat to verify your system catalogs, 
it checks for invalid indexes that need rebuilding, and it initializes 
a temporary 4.0 system.

*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

<old_GPHOME_path>
 
  Required. The absolute path to the current version of Greenplum 
  Database software you want to migrate away from.


<new_GPHOME_path>

  Required. The absolute path to the new version of Greenplum 
  Database software you want to migrate to.


--replication_port_base <port>
 
  Required if upgrading a system with mirrors. Specifies the base 
  number by which the port numbers for the primary file replication 
  process are calculated. The first replication port on a host is set 
  as REPLICATION_PORT_BASE, and then incremented by one for each 
  additional primary segment on that host. Valid values range from 
  1 through 65535 and cannot conflict with other port assignments 
  on a segment host.


--mirror_replication_port_base <port>

  Required if upgrading a system with mirrors. Specifies the base 
  number by which the port numbers for the mirror file replication 
  process are calculated. The first mirror replication port on a 
  host is set as MIRROR_REPLICATION_PORT_BASE, and then incremented 
  by one for each additional mirror segment on that host. Valid values 
  range from 1 through 65535 and cannot conflict with other port 
  assignments on a segment host.


--mirror-mode redundant | single

  Specifies how to upgrade mirror segments to the new 4.0 format. 
  Redundant mode will keep a copy of the old 3.3 mirror until the new 
  mirror is fully synchronized. Redundant mode always ensures that the 
  system always has a valid mirror copy for every segment, and is the 
  default and preferred mode provided that your systems have enough 
  available disk space. Single mode removes the old mirror before creating 
  the new mirror on the target node, and can be used on systems where disk 
  space is limited.


--nosnapshot

  (Solaris only) On systems that support zfs snapshots the default 
  behavior is to pause the upgrade after the mirrors have been 
  synchronized, but before the upgraded catalog has been installed. This 
  allows users to take a zfs snapshot before upgrade progresses beyond 
  the point of no return. The --nosnapshot option runs the complete upgrade 
  as a single step, just as it would do on systems that do not support zfs.


--resume

  (Solaris only) On systems that support zfs snapshots, allows the upgrade 
  process to resume where it left off after it has been paused to allow 
  for a zfs snapshot to be taken.


--prepare

  Optional. Running gpmigrator with the --prepare option will perform 
  some pre-upgrade validation, but not execute the upgrade. The prepare 
  phase does three things; it runs a utility called gpcheckcat to verify 
  your system catalogs, it checks for invalid indexes that need rebuilding, 
  and it initializes a temporary 4.0 system. 


--master_port <port>
--port_base <port>
--mirror_port_base <port>
--replication_port_base <port>
--mirror_replication_port_base <port>

  Optional. Used with the --prepare option to supply port assignments 
  for a temporary 4.0 system to be initialized. These ports cannot 
  conflict with your current system’s port assignments. 


-R (revert)
 
  Restores the backup directory in the event of a failed upgrade. 
  This is only available if running in redundant mode (mirror-mode=redundant).
  If the migration procedure fails and you cannot start the system 
  using the binary files of the prior version, it is probable that gpmigrator 
  was not able to revert automatically. In these cases, you can run 
  gpmigrator -R to attempt to restore the backup directory. 
  Cases in which the migration is not automatically reverted include:
  * The gpmigrator process is killed with a command such as kill -9
  * The system crashes during the migration procedure
  * You are running gpmigrator in mirror-mode=single mode
  If a ZFS snapshot is available, backing up from those resources is preferred.


-d <master_data_directory>

  Optional. The current master host data directory. If not specified, 
  the value set for $MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.


-l <logfile_directory>

 The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-q (quiet mode)
 
 Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen, 
 but is still written to the log file.


--help | -h

  Displays the online help.


--debug

  Sets logging level to debug.


--version | -v

  Displays the version of this utility. 


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Upgrade to version 4.0 from version 3.3.6.1 (make sure you are 
using the 4.0 version of gpmigrator):

/usr/local/greenplum-db-4.0.0.0/bin/gpmigrator \
/usr/local/greenplum-db-3.3.6.1 \
/usr/local/greenplum-db-4.0.0.0 \
--replication_port_base 53000 \
--mirror_replication_port_base 54000 \
--mirror-mode redundant


Resume an upgrade that has been paused for a snapshot (make sure you 
are using the 4.0 version of gpmigrator):

/usr/local/greenplum-db-4.0.0.0/bin/gpmigrator \
/usr/local/greenplum-db-3.3.6.1 \
/usr/local/greenplum-db-4.0.0.0 --resume


